---
layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: merit-calculator
title: "Merit & Aggregate Calculator (Custom Weights)"
description: "Calculate your admission merit aggregate from Matric, Intermediate, entry test and interview marks, with custom weights or verified NUST and MDCAT presets."
permalink: /tools/merit-calculator/
h1: "Merit & Aggregate Calculator"
extraCss: "/tools/merit-calculator/style.css"
quickAnswer: "Aggregate = Σ (marks obtained ÷ total marks × weight %). With Matric 900/1100 at 10%, Intermediate 950/1100 at 40% and an entry test 300/400 at 50%, the aggregate is 8.18 + 34.55 + 37.50 = 80.23%. The weights must add up to 100%, and every university sets its own, so confirm them in the official prospectus for your admission year."
formula:
  title: "Merit Aggregate Formula"
  expression: "Aggregate % = Σ (Obtained ÷ Total × 100 × Weight ÷ 100)"
  explanation: "Each component (Matric/SSC, Intermediate/HSSC, entry test, interview and so on) is first turned into a percentage of its own total, so a test out of 200 and a board exam out of 1100 can be compared. That percentage is multiplied by the component's weight, and the weighted parts are added. Because the weights total 100%, the result is itself a percentage from 0 to 100. The calculator keeps full precision until the end and rounds the aggregate to 2 decimal places."
  variables:
    - name: "Obtained / Total"
      description: "Your marks in that component and the maximum possible, exactly as on your result card."
    - name: "Weight"
      description: "The share of the aggregate the institution gives that component, in %. All weights together must equal 100%."
    - name: "Contribution"
      description: "Your percentage in the component × its weight ÷ 100: the points it adds to your aggregate."
workedExamples:
  - title: "Matric, Intermediate and entry test (10 / 40 / 50)"
    inputs:
      - "Matric: 900 / 1100, weight 10%"
      - "Intermediate: 950 / 1100, weight 40%"
      - "Entry test: 300 / 400, weight 50%"
    steps:
      - "Matric: 900 ÷ 1100 = 81.818%, × 10% = 8.1818"
      - "Intermediate: 950 ÷ 1100 = 86.364%, × 40% = 34.5455"
      - "Entry test: 300 ÷ 400 = 75%, × 50% = 37.5"
      - "Total: 8.1818 + 34.5455 + 37.5 = 80.2273"
    result: "Aggregate 80.23%"
    link: "/tools/merit-calculator/?preset=custom&c1n=Matric&c1o=900&c1t=1100&c1w=10&c2n=Intermediate&c2o=950&c2t=1100&c2w=40&c3n=Entry+test&c3o=300&c3t=400&c3w=50#tool"
  - title: "NUST NET weights (10 / 15 / 75)"
    inputs:
      - "SSC: 1000 / 1100, weight 10%"
      - "HSSC Part-1: 480 / 550, weight 15%"
      - "NET: 140 / 200, weight 75%"
    steps:
      - "SSC: 90.909% × 10% = 9.0909"
      - "HSSC: 87.273% × 15% = 13.0909"
      - "NET: 70% × 75% = 52.5"
    result: "Aggregate 74.68%"
    link: "/tools/merit-calculator/?preset=nust-net&c1o=1000&c1t=1100&c2o=480&c2t=550&c3o=140&c3t=200#tool"
  - title: "With an interview (four components)"
    inputs:
      - "SSC 850/1100 at 10%, HSSC 900/1100 at 30%"
      - "Test 150/200 at 50%, interview 40/50 at 10%"
    steps:
      - "SSC 77.273% × 10% = 7.7273; HSSC 81.818% × 30% = 24.5455"
      - "Test 75% × 50% = 37.5; interview 80% × 10% = 8"
    result: "Aggregate 77.77%"
    link: "/tools/merit-calculator/?preset=custom&c1n=SSC&c1o=850&c1t=1100&c1w=10&c2n=HSSC&c2o=900&c2t=1100&c2w=30&c3n=Test&c3o=150&c3t=200&c3w=50&c4n=Interview&c4o=40&c4t=50&c4w=10#tool"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Using last year's weights"
    description: "Universities and regulators change their merit formulas, sometimes every year. Always check the weights in the official prospectus or admission notice for the session you are applying to."
  - title: "Adding raw marks instead of percentages"
    description: "Adding 900 board marks to 150 test marks gives a number that means nothing, because the totals differ. Each component must be turned into a percentage of its own total before it is weighted."
  - title: "Entering the wrong total"
    description: "Board totals differ between Matric schemes and boards, and some formulas use HSSC Part-1 marks only. Copy the total exactly from your result card and from the institution's rules."
  - title: "Forgetting hafiz-e-Quran or other additions"
    description: "Some institutions add extra marks or deduct marks for repeated attempts. These rules vary, so add them yourself according to the official prospectus."
  - title: "Rounding each component"
    description: "Rounding every contribution before adding can move the final aggregate by a few hundredths, which matters on close merit lists. The calculator keeps full precision and rounds only the final result."
faqs:
  - q: "How do I calculate my merit aggregate?"
    a: "Divide your marks by the total marks for each component, multiply by that component's weight, and add the results. For 900/1100 at 10%, 950/1100 at 40% and 300/400 at 50% the aggregate is 80.23%."
  - q: "What is the NUST aggregate formula?"
    a: "For admission on NET basis, NUST's published criteria are NET 75%, HSSC (Part-1 or full) 15% and SSC/O Level 10%. Choose the NUST preset, enter your marks, and check NUST's merit criteria page for the current session."
  - q: "What is the MDCAT aggregate formula?"
    a: "Under the PM&DC Admissions, Curriculum and Conduct Regulations 2026, open merit admission to public and private medical and dental colleges uses MDCAT 50%, FSc/HSSC 40% and SSC/Matric 10%. Your admitting university publishes the final criteria."
  - q: "Why does the calculator say my weights must total 100%?"
    a: "An aggregate is a weighted percentage, so the weights must cover the whole 100%. If they add up to 90%, a component or part of a weight is missing; check the institution's formula."
  - q: "Can I add an interview or a second test?"
    a: "Yes. Custom mode accepts up to five components. Give each a name, marks obtained, total marks and weight."
  - q: "Where are the weights for UET ECAT or other universities?"
    a: "This calculator only includes presets that were checked against an official source. For any other university, use Custom mode and copy the weights from its current prospectus."
  - q: "Is my calculated aggregate the official one?"
    a: "No. It is an estimate from the marks and weights you enter. The admitting institution's own calculation and merit list are final."
sources:
  - name: "NUST: Merit generation criteria for UG admission on NET basis"
    url: "https://nust.edu.pk/admissions/undergraduates/merit-criteria-for-admission-on-net-basis/"
  - name: "PM&DC: Laws and regulations (Admissions, Curriculum and Conduct Regulations 2026)"
    url: "https://pmdc.pk/Publication/LawsAndRegulations"
  - name: "IBCC: Equivalence of O/A Level and foreign qualifications"
    url: "https://ibcc.edu.pk/"
lastReviewed: "2026-10-31"
howto:
  name: "How to Calculate a Merit Aggregate"
  description: "Combine board results, entry tests and interviews into an admission aggregate."
  steps:
    - name: "Choose custom or a preset"
      text: "Use Custom to type any weights, or pick a verified preset to fill in its components and weights."
    - name: "Enter your marks"
      text: "For each component, type marks obtained and total marks exactly as on your result card."
    - name: "Check the weights"
      text: "Make sure the weights add up to 100% and match the official prospectus for your admission year."
    - name: "Read your aggregate"
      text: "See each component's percentage and contribution, and your aggregate to two decimal places."
---
<script type="application/json" id="merit-presets">{{ meritPresets | dump | safe }}</script>

    <!-- Tool Form Container -->
    <section class="tool-container" aria-label="Merit and Aggregate Calculator">
      <form id="calculator-form" novalidate>
        <div class="form-group merit-preset">
          <label for="preset">Weights</label>
          <select id="preset" data-param="preset" class="form-control" aria-describedby="preset-note">
            <option value="custom" selected>Custom (enter your own weights)</option>
            {% for p in meritPresets %}
            <option value="{{ p.id }}">{{ p.name }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>
        <p class="form-hint" id="preset-note">Weights differ between universities and can change each year. Confirm them in the official prospectus for your admission session.</p>
        {% for p in meritPresets %}
        <div class="merit-preset-note" data-preset-note="{{ p.id }}" hidden>
          <p>{{ p.institution }}. {{ p.note }}</p>
          <p>Source: <a href="{{ p.source }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{{ p.sourceName }}</a> · verified <time datetime="{{ p.verifiedOn }}">{{ p.verifiedOn | humanDate }}</time>. Weights can change each year; confirm them in the official prospectus.</p>
        </div>
        {% endfor %}

        <div class="merit-rows" role="group" aria-label="Components">
          <div class="merit-head" aria-hidden="true"><span>Component</span><span>Obtained</span><span>Total</span><span>Weight %</span></div>
          {% for i in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] %}
          <div class="merit-row" data-row="{{ i }}">
            <div class="merit-cell merit-name">
              <label class="merit-label" for="c{{ i }}-name">Component {{ i }}</label>
              <input type="text" id="c{{ i }}-name" data-param="c{{ i }}n" class="form-control comp-name" autocomplete="off" placeholder="{{ ['e.g. Matric', 'e.g. Intermediate', 'e.g. Entry test', 'e.g. Interview', 'Optional'][loop.index0] }}">
            </div>
            <div class="merit-cell">
              <label class="merit-label" for="c{{ i }}-obtained">Obtained</label>
              <input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="any" min="0" id="c{{ i }}-obtained" data-param="c{{ i }}o" class="form-control comp-obtained">
            </div>
            <div class="merit-cell">
              <label class="merit-label" for="c{{ i }}-total">Total</label>
              <input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="any" min="0" id="c{{ i }}-total" data-param="c{{ i }}t" class="form-control comp-total">
            </div>
            <div class="merit-cell">
              <label class="merit-label" for="c{{ i }}-weight">Weight %</label>
              <input type="number" inputmode="decimal" step="any" min="0" max="100" id="c{{ i }}-weight" data-param="c{{ i }}w" class="form-control comp-weight">
            </div>
          </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
        <p class="form-hint merit-weight-total" id="weight-total" aria-live="polite"></p>

        <div class="button-group mt-6">
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Calculate Aggregate</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" id="reset-btn">Reset</button>
        </div>
      </form>

      <div id="error-box" class="hidden form-error" role="alert"></div>

      <div id="result-box" class="result-box is-visible" aria-live="polite">
        <div class="result-header">
          <span id="result-header-text">Aggregate</span>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost" id="copy-result-btn" title="Copy result to clipboard" aria-label="Copy result to clipboard">
            <span data-icon="copy"></span> Copy Result
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="result-primary" id="result-primary-text">--</div>
        <div class="table-wrapper merit-table-wrapper">
          <table class="table merit-table">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">Each component's percentage and contribution to the aggregate</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col" class="wide-only">Marks</th><th scope="col">%</th><th scope="col" class="wide-only">Weight</th><th scope="col">Adds</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody id="merit-table-rows"></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="merit-steps-title">Working</p>
        <ol class="merit-steps" id="result-steps"></ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- tool-end -->

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Always Check the Official Prospectus</h2>
      <p>Merit formulas are set by each university, medical regulator or admitting authority, and they change. Weights shift between Matric, Intermediate and entry tests; some institutions use HSSC Part-1 marks only, add an interview, or treat O/A Level equivalence differently. Before you rely on any aggregate, open the official prospectus or admission notice for your session and compare its formula with the weights you entered.</p>
      <p>This calculator includes presets only where the weights were checked against an official source, and each preset shows that source and the date it was checked:</p>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Verified merit presets</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Preset</th><th scope="col">Weights</th><th scope="col">Verified</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {% for p in meritPresets %}
            <tr>
              <td><a href="{{ p.source }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{{ p.name }}</a></td>
              <td>{% for c in p.components %}{{ c.name }} {{ c.weight }}%{% if not loop.last %} · {% endif %}{% endfor %}</td>
              <td><time datetime="{{ p.verifiedOn }}">{{ p.verifiedOn | humanDate }}</time></td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>For any other university, choose Custom and copy the weights from its prospectus. If the formula includes something this calculator does not, such as extra marks for hafiz-e-Quran or a deduction for an improvement attempt, apply that adjustment to your marks first.</p>

      <h2>Improving Your Aggregate</h2>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Look at the weights:</strong> a component with a 50% or 75% weight moves your aggregate far more than one at 10%. Under NUST's formula, each extra NET percentage point adds 0.75 to your aggregate.</li>
        <li><strong>Know what is fixed:</strong> your Matric marks are usually final by the time you apply; your entry test and sometimes your Intermediate marks are not.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare with past merit:</strong> institutions often publish closing merit from previous years. Treat them as a rough guide only; they change with the number of applicants and seats.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
